# Environment
.venv/

# Claude Code local machine state
.claude/settings.local.json

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Generated targets: the TIFFs are large and reproducible from the sidecar,
# so the sidecar JSON (seed + geometry + patch values) is what gets tracked.
targets/*.tif
targets/*.tiff

# Processed negatives. Large, and reproducible from the source positive plus the
# profile that made them, so the recipe is tracked and the output is not.
out/
*_negative.tif

# The supplied 256-patch chart. Untracked because its provenance is unknown and it is not
# ours to redistribute; it stays on the working machine. tests/conftest.py skips the chart
# test cleanly when it is absent, so a fresh clone still runs green.
EDN_RGB_256.tif
EDN_RGB_256_2.tif

# Scans of calibration prints. Tens of MB each and one per calibration sheet.
#
# Unlike everything else here these are *not* reproducible — they are measurements of a
# physical print, and once deleted the numbers cannot be re-derived. What gets tracked is
# the conclusion: the profile records the measured blocker, the density range, the scan
# settings and the filename it came from. If the analysis maths is ever revised and the
# raw sheets are wanted again, they have to have been archived outside git.
*Blocker*.tif
*Wedge*.tif
*Exposure*.tif
*_scan.tif

# Photographs, negatives and scans of finished prints — everything image-shaped.
#
# Deliberately a blanket rule rather than a list of the ones seen so far. `git add -A` once
# swept 200MB of Steven's own photographs into a commit, and what caught it was GitHub's
# file-size limit rather than anything here. A rule that only names known files fails
# exactly when a new one appears, which is the moment it matters. Anything genuinely
# belonging in the repository can be added with `git add -f`.
*.tif
*.tiff
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.png
